/** worker-slot-7 — siman 299 fixes from output (16 blocks) */
export const FIXES = {
  "beer-hagolah/part-001.txt": {
    "1:ב":
      "(ב) Semag, positive commandments, siman 59 per the Rif's view and Mahari Kolin in root 96.",
  },
  "biur-halacha/part-001.txt": {
    "1:א":
      "When it becomes dark — see in Mishna Berurah that this is even in a case of doubt about darkness; so too Magen Avraham and Bach, and Ma'amar Mordechai elaborated on this and also agreed to the law with them, and not like the view of Taz who leans to be lenient to rely on the view of Rosh. But in truth it is very difficult to rely on this after many later authorities hold that even the view of Rosh is to be strict in a case of doubt about darkness [see in Ma'amar Mordechai and Turei Even on Shabbat and Peri Megadim], and nothing remains for us except the view of Razah who is alone in this. And even if we say the Rosh also holds like him to be lenient as Taz, behold the Rif and Rambam [for Rambam wrote explicitly regarding kiddush that from the time he sanctified the day it is forbidden and he must stop, and it is known in the Gemara that havdalah for beginning is equivalent to kiddush for stopping] and Semag and Milchamot [and see there that so too is the view of R' Hai Gaon] and Ittur all hold that in bein haShemashot too it is forbidden to begin, and only regarding if he already began to eat while it is still day do many early authorities permit in this not to stop when it becomes dark — they are Rambam, Rosh, and Razah, and also the view of Rif [as Ran explained and Gra also compelled that so is his view] is thus, and the strict ones in this remain only the view of Semag and Ittur; therefore the Mechaber mentioned their words only as some say and the first view anonymously. We return to our matter that from the essence of the law one may not be lenient in a case of doubt about darkness, and so is proven in Beit Yosef for one who examines it. And that which he mentioned in Shulchan Aruch is not to diminish a case of doubt about darkness [see in Milchamot that he brought several places that the intent is even a case of doubt about darkness]; or alternatively because of the latter part he wrote that even if it became dark he need not stop. And behold at great wedding feasts the custom in several places is to be lenient and to remain seated in a case of doubt about darkness, and Bach wondered at this; it appears that in a place of pressure they rely on Razah, and one may not protest against them [R' Akiva Eiger]. And further it appears to me to somewhat justify their custom because it is only a rabbinic concern and it is in a place of mitzvah they ruled leniently like R' Yosi on Shabbat 35 that his time of bein haShemashot is later than the bein haShemashot of R' Yehudah.",
    "5:_":
      "One may make havdalah on Shabbat while it is still day — see in Peri Megadim who wrote that here it deals with one who did not accept the fast upon himself at Minchah on Shabbat, for then once he made havdalah it would be forbidden to eat; but he had already accepted upon himself to fast three days from Sunday eve, and he inferred this from Magen Avraham from what he wrote that it deals with one who vowed to fast every Sunday three days, etc. And in my humble opinion it appears Magen Avraham does not intend this but only to refute the words of Olat Shabbat who understood in the words of Levush that there was no acceptance at all before havdalah, and therefore wrote on him \"I do not know what he is saying\" see there [the reason for this is that before Peri Megadim there was no book Olat Shabbat, as he himself wrote they did not have it except from siman 319 and onward]. But for the law there is no difference whether he accepted at Minchah, and it is not comparable to Tish'a BeAv and Yom Kippur where there it is an obligatory fast in essence, therefore it is strict that he cannot make havdalah while it is still day because that is considered acceptance and it would be forbidden for him to drink afterwards. And even if we say Magen Avraham's intent was to be precise as Peri Megadim wrote, nevertheless for the law it appears one need not be strict in this because I found explicitly in R' Akiva Eiger that here it deals with one who already accepted the fast at Minchah and even so he is permitted to drink from the cup of havdalah since it is still certainly day, and so too Ma'amar Mordechai explicitly as I will quote his language below.",
  },
  "levushei-serad/part-001.txt": {
    "6:_":
      "(s.k. 2) siman 271 seif 4 — meaning: just as where he brought in the previous s.k. proof from there that it is forbidden to begin, so too it is reasonable to say one must even stop in the middle of the meal by having prayed.",
  },
  "machatzit-hashekel/part-001.txt": {
    "1:א":
      "(s.k. 1) When it becomes dark, etc. — even a case of doubt about darkness. In Pesachim daf 105a they say in the name of Rav: Shabbat establishes itself for kiddush (meaning when he stands in the middle of eating and sanctified the day he must stop), but for havdalah it does not establish, and they say this is only for stopping that he need not stop his eating and make havdalah, but to begin is forbidden. And that which he need not stop — this is when he is eating; but when he did not establish himself for eating but only for drinking he must stop. And Rif holds that that which they say he need not stop is specifically in a case of doubt about darkness, but in certain darkness he must stop even from eating, and this is the view of some brought here in Shulchan Aruch; and Rosh wrote on this: \"These are not sensible words and far from reason to say the give-and-take of the Talmud, etc., deals specifically with a case of doubt about darkness\" — end of Rosh's words; behold the view of Rosh is that even in certain darkness he need not stop from eating, and this is one view here in Shulchan Aruch. And Taz holds that Rif and Rosh disagree in two matters: one regarding stopping, for Rif in certain darkness must stop and for Rosh he need not stop; second, since for Rif the Talmud deals with a case of doubt about darkness, therefore that which the Talmud says \"this is only for stopping but to begin is forbidden\" means even in a case of doubt about darkness it is forbidden to begin, which is not so for Rosh where the Talmud deals with certain darkness but a case of doubt about darkness is even permitted to begin. But this is not Magen Avraham's view, for regarding beginning Rosh agrees that even in a case of doubt about darkness it is forbidden; for otherwise why did the Mechaber in Shulchan Aruch not mention the dispute of Rif and Rosh except regarding stopping — he should also have mentioned their dispute regarding beginning. Or the Beit Yosef holds Rosh agrees it is forbidden to begin even in a case of doubt about darkness, and Beit Yosef's proof is from Rosh's words who wrote on Rif's view \"these are not sensible words, etc., the give-and-take deals specifically with a case of doubt about darkness\" — if so, the same applies to Rosh's method that it is strained to say the Talmud deals specifically with certain darkness. Or Rosh holds the Talmud deals in all cases whether doubt or certain darkness: for stopping even certain darkness he need not, and for beginning even a case of doubt about darkness is forbidden; and so is Bach's view. If so, now it is settled that what Shulchan Aruch wrote \"when it becomes dark\" is not certain darkness but the same applies to a case of doubt about darkness, and I already wrote all this above siman 271 s.k. 10.",
    "2:א":
      "(s.k. 2) He need not, etc. — and it is possible that if he prayed, etc. — meaning: in s.k. 1 it is obvious to Magen Avraham that if he prayed it is forbidden to eat even while it is still day, namely if he did not begin in permitted time; what is different here is that he began in permitted time and stopped in the middle and prayed — about this he is uncertain.",
    "6:ד":
      "Behold regarding drinking all agree, etc. — so it should read; meaning: why did Mahariik tie the matter to Rif and Rosh in seif 1 — behold even per Rosh in seif 1 he need not stop from eating even in certain darkness, nevertheless regarding drinking he agrees one must stop, for so is explicit in the Talmud there: \"this is for eating but for drinking they stop.\" And Magen Avraham added even in a case of doubt about darkness; Magen Avraham follows his method in s.k. 1 that for Rosh the teaching must deal in all cases whether certain darkness or doubt about darkness; so Bach himself explained. If so, on both of them the Talmud said \"but for drinking they stop,\" meaning even in a case of doubt about darkness.",
    "6:ו":
      "It deals with drinking after the meal — meaning they certainly first reclined for eating, and in the middle of the meal he stopped for havdalah and again ceased eating and established themselves to drink alone; therefore he did not need to bless Birkat haMazon. And it appears to me as the simple meaning, etc., and as written in seif 8: when he made havdalah even after netilat yadayim, then he exempts with Borei peri haGafen of havdalah the wine within the meal, and there is no longer wine neither within the meal nor for Birkat haMazon. Some say he must recite the after-blessing on the wine of havdalah, and Birkat haMazon does not exempt it even though Birkat haMazon exempts the after-blessing for wine before the meal. And similarly Birkat haMazon exempts the after-blessing on the kiddush cup even though he has no cup for Birkat haMazon — because wine before the meal pertains to the meal, and similarly kiddush since kiddush is only in the context of a meal; not so havdalah which has no connection to the meal, therefore Birkat haMazon does not exempt it. And similarly in siman 174 seif 4, and so in this siman seif 7: some say if he made havdalah before netilat yadayim, the havdalah does not exempt the wine within the meal from Borei peri haGafen, and kiddush before netilat yadayim exempts the wine within the meal — also because kiddush has connection to the meal unlike havdalah; therefore some here too hold it is weaker in this regard even than kiddush, that havdalah within the meal requires Borei peri haGafen since it has no connection to the meal. And at first glance this explanation is insufficient to justify Mahariik's view, for Mahariik tied the reason per Rif and Semak who hold in certain darkness one must stop even from eating, and Magen Avraham's reason is not stated; even per Rosh who argues on Rif, Mahariik did not come to justify only the Mechaber's view in Shulchan Aruch. And some say one must bless because Shulchan Aruch did not intend Mahariik but the view in seif 8 and siman 174; perhaps Magen Avraham's intents also justify Mahariik's view, and he holds Magen Avraham's reasoning alone is insufficient to require Borei peri haGafen on the havdalah cup except combined with the view of Rif and Semag.",
    "9:מ":
      "Therefore it appears to me, etc., and he drinks as they do at a wedding feast, as written in Even HaEzer siman 62: he blesses in Birkat haMazon on one cup and afterwards takes a second cup and blesses on it the six blessings, then returns and takes the first cup and blesses on it Borei peri haGafen which is one of the seven wedding blessings; and there too the reason is that two sanctifications cannot be on one cup. And so he should do here: he sanctifies on one cup and does not bless on it Borei peri haGafen, for if he blesses, since he is not permitted to taste before havdalah and havdalah would be an interruption between Borei peri haGafen and drinking, therefore he does not bless Borei peri haGafen on the kiddush cup but kiddush alone; afterwards he takes the havdalah cup and blesses Borei peri haGafen and then makes havdalah on it and exempts with Borei peri haGafen also the kiddush cup — now havdalah is not an interruption, for so is his way all year. Or he makes havdalah also without Borei peri haGafen and after havdalah blesses Borei peri haGafen on both cups, and it is exactly like weddings.",
    "9:ע":
      "For some say it is permitted, etc. — meaning here it was permitted for him to eat the hamotzi before havdalah since he is compelled, for we find a similar case: some say in siman 296 seif 3 if he has no wine and beer, even if he expects to have tomorrow, nevertheless he is permitted to eat at night before havdalah. And although Tosafot and Rosh daf 106b s.v. mekadesh, etc. wrote for those who stop that when he has only beer he must nevertheless sanctify on bread and not on beer — if so when Yom Tov falls on Motza'ei Shabbat he must say yotzer oroh, kiddush, havdalah, borei peri hagafen, me'ein sheva, blessing of the day, and hamazon, and since one does not make havdalah on bread, it is a wonder how they say half yotzer oroh on bread and half on beer — namely on Motza'ei Shabbat on Yom Tov where both are one matter as stated; but here they agree this matter does not apply to the other. But there is a dispute whether he blesses on beer as written in seif 3; and some say if he tasted he does not make havdalah; therefore it is better to do kiddush also on one cup of beer as stated regarding wine, and as written in siman 296 seif 3, for even Rosh who holds one does not sanctify on beer, as written in siman 272 seif 9, nevertheless on Motza'ei Shabbat on Yom Tov it is better to do both on beer. Or he hears kiddush from others and the one who leads does not taste until after he makes havdalah on beer and drinks, because it is forbidden to eat before havdalah, and afterwards the leader blesses hamotzi and eats, fulfilling kiddush even without tasting yet; see siman 167 seif 6 and in Hagahah, and what is written in siman 271 seif 6.",
  },
  "magen-avraham/part-001.txt": {
    "6:ב":
      "(ב) While it is still day. And we do not say that since he made havdalah he accepted the fast upon himself — only regarding an obligatory fast do we say thus, as on Yom Kippur or Tish'a BeAv, and so explicitly in the Gemara; and Olat Shabbat did not know this and challenged Levush incorrectly, and they are the very words of Terumat haDeshen, and it deals with one who vowed to fast every Sunday three days and three nights, or on every Rosh Chodesh and the like — see there siman 556. But here regarding vows we follow the language of people that the fast does not begin until night [there in Terumat haDeshen], and it implies he prays Ma'ariv before he makes havdalah and even so he is permitted to eat afterwards; and the same on a weekday — he is permitted to eat after Ma'ariv while it is still day, but in any case he prays Ma'ariv before havdalah, and so from what is written in siman 293 that because of havdalah he also prays Ma'ariv while it is still day; and siman 293 seif 3 see there in the Gemara that prayer is obvious and havdalah is in question — if so where havdalah is permitted, all the more so prayer. Shelah wrote: one who fasts two days and two nights before Rosh Hashanah makes havdalah on the exit of day two, and if Rosh Hashanah fell on day three he should not wait until night three, for then he would need to make havdalah with yotzer oroh, kiddush, havdalah, and it would be astonishing; and it appears to me that if it happened he waited he should not say kiddush and havdalah on one cup, for they say in Pesachim daf 102 that one cannot have two kiddushot on one cup, except kiddush and havdalah because they are one matter; and Rashi explained both are because of sanctity of the festival — in havdalah itself he mentions sanctity of the festival, for \"who separates between holy and holy\" he blesses — end quote; and so Rashbam. But havdalah of weekday has no such matter. Greater than this Tosafot wrote s.v. venimrinhu: for both on one cup, etc., that even per the questioner's view who holds one says two kiddushot on one cup, nevertheless if it refers to a weekday meal and kiddush refers to Shabbat, one cannot have both on one cup; and one might say even now he should say between holy and holy, which would be false; therefore it appears he should sanctify on one cup and afterwards make havdalah on another cup and drink as they do at wedding feasts. If he has no wine he sanctifies on bread and eats hamotzi and afterwards makes havdalah on beer, for some say it is permitted to eat before havdalah as written in siman 296 seif 3; and although Tosafot and Rosh chapter 99 wrote it is a wonder how they say half yotzer oroh on bread and half on beer — namely on Motza'ei Shabbat on Yom Tov where both are one matter as stated — but here they agree this matter does not apply; however there is a dispute whether he blesses on beer as in seif 3, and some say if he tasted he does not make havdalah; therefore it is better to do kiddush also on one cup of beer as with wine, and as in siman 296 seif 3, for even Rosh who holds one does not sanctify on beer, as in siman 272 seif 9, nevertheless on Motza'ei Shabbat on Yom Tov it is better to do both on beer. Or he hears kiddush from others and the leader does not taste until after havdalah on beer; because it is forbidden to eat before havdalah, and afterwards he blesses hamotzi and eats, fulfilling kiddush without tasting yet; see siman 167 seif 6 and Hagahah, and siman 271 seif 6.",
  },
  "mechaber/part-001.txt": {
    "6:main":
      "(10) If one forgot and did not make havdalah on Motza'ei Shabbat, (16) he may make havdalah until the end of day 3. (20) And there are those who say he does not make havdalah except all day Sunday and no more, and specifically on Borei peri haGafen and who separates between holy and profane. (21) But on candle and spices he does not bless except on Motza'ei Shabbat. (23) And there is one who says that what we established that one who tasted may still make havdalah — this applies where he made havdalah at night on Motza'ei Shabbat; but if he did not make havdalah at night, once he tasted he may no longer make havdalah. {Rama: The essence is like the first opinion. And one who fasts three days and three nights hears havdalah from others. And if there are no others near him he may make havdalah on Shabbat while it is still day and drink, and afterwards accept the fast upon himself. [Terumat haDeshen siman 159] See siman 553.}",
    "7:main":
      "(50) One who makes havdalah over wine (24) at his table — even if he made havdalah before washing his hands, he exempts the wine within the meal so he need not bless on it. (25) And there are those who say he is not exempt unless he washed his hands before he made havdalah. {Rama: And if he made havdalah first he must afterwards recite the blessing of me'ein shelish. [Tosafot and Mordechai chapter Keitzad Mevarchin]}",
  },
  "mishnah-berurah/part-001.txt": {
    "5:_":
      "(15) If he erred and ate, etc. — and the same if he acted intentionally and ate: even though he transgressed intentionally, nevertheless he must make havdalah afterwards at night; only the usual manner of speech was used. And similarly what he wrote afterwards in seif 6 that he forgot and did not make havdalah — the same applies if he intentionally did not make havdalah.",
  },
  "peri-megadim/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_":
      "When it becomes dark — Eshel Avraham Pesachim 105a; and Rif's text reads \"if it became light\" — Yoma says you do not need, etc., and a case of doubt about darkness you do not need to examine whether it is dark or not dark to establish, and since in a case of doubt about darkness they spread a cloth and sanctified and returned to their meal see there; and from Ran's words there it implies they asked whether it became light on the day of bein haShemashot — similar to light that shines in bein haShemashot not (siman 381 in Taz and Magen Avraham, and it would be close like immediately at the beginning of bein haShemashot, explained there whether so) — and they are permitted to sanctify; he said you do not need, for on Yom Kippur too they may sanctify, etc., and therefore it implies that before Yom Kippur it is forbidden to sanctify for his time has not yet arrived; and Rav prayed Minchah while it is still day — prayer is different, for kiddush on the cup is specifically in bein haShemashot — see Ma'amar Mordechai siman 67a; nevertheless per this, Shabbat establishes itself in bein haShemashot for kiddush and it is forbidden to eat in bein haShemashot before kiddush, and for havdalah in bein haShemashot it is not fitting to make havdalah without a cup, to take out the day from others, but regarding forbidding eating he said for havdalah he told him to stop in bein haShemashot; and there is no resolution to say for Rif certain darkness stops for havdalah, only Beit Yosef Semag in the name of Rif so too. But Rosh brought thus in the name of Ba'al haIttur that Rav does not argue on Shmuel and rejected his words see there. However from Rambam's words there I learned another explanation: certainly regarding that one may sanctify from Minchah onward and relies on that kiddush at night — only they asked whether it became light on the day to establish by uprooting the table that Shabbat's meal should be recognizable; he said you do not need, whether for uprooting the table or for what they hold in bein haShemashot it is permitted to eat, for immediately in bein haShemashot it is forbidden to eat and he must stop, and he need not also uproot the table because Shabbat establishes itself for its honor, and similarly Rashi and Rashbam except their text reads \"if he sanctifies the day in bein haShemashot.\" And Rosh brought the words of Ba'al haMaor who said you need not hurry in bein haShemashot but at bein haShemashot like ma'aser which is permitted in bein haShemashot — casual eating on Shabbat; and in Milchamot I understood from his words that Ba'al haMaor permits eating in bein haShemashot before kiddush, and he challenged him from what is taught Pesachim 102a: they were sitting and sanctified the day — R' Yehudah says they sanctify, R' Yosi says they eat, etc., — apparently per R' Yehudah in bein haShemashot it is forbidden to eat; but from Rosh I understood Ba'al haMaor certainly agrees that in bein haShemashot it is forbidden to eat before kiddush, only they asked if he sanctifies the day in bein haShemashot should he stop and the meal of Shabbat be established, namely by uprooting the table, and sanctify the sanctification of the day; he said he need not hurry in kiddush and uprooting the table in bein haShemashot, or spreading a cloth in honor of Shabbat, only from darkness he must as in the incident specifically he must — thus for stopping from eating certainly he stops in bein haShemashot as proven from the braitta 102a see there, for sanctifying the day is certainly in bein haShemashot even per one who has no twilight, and so Taz wrote there is a difference between Rif and Rosh: he brought Ba'al haMaor's words that certainly darkness is the case, and from that for havdalah he need not stop in certain darkness if he already began, but to begin is forbidden; in a case of doubt about darkness one may say it is permitted for havdalah. But Magen Avraham siman 1 and 3 holds like Bach that even for Rosh it is forbidden to begin havdalah in bein haShemashot, since he said it is understood that just as it establishes for kiddush so for havdalah he said for havdalah it does not establish — these matters are for stopping, but we do not begin; these matters are where there is a difference in stopping, and here too at the beginning there is a difference, for in kiddush in bein haShemashot he stops, but havdalah in bein haShemashot is permitted — it implies at the beginning there is no difference between kiddush and havdalah at all, and in bein haShemashot it is forbidden to begin havdalah like kiddush even per Rosh. And behold for the first view which is primary, that Rav says so — nevertheless it is forbidden to eat casually in Israel where there is no tithe lest, as Rashi and Rashbam explained, examine there. And one need not elaborate.",
    "9:_":
      "For example — Eshel Avraham was somewhat lenient in this and Magen Avraham siman 17 was strict in this see there. Wine of havdalah and kiddush — if he vowed, it applies to him as explained in Yoreh De'ah siman 235 and 239 that it applies; and siman 272 seif 9 see there in Magen Avraham on this.",
  },
};
